  "_title": "The control ROM — a control word you can program",
  "_description": "The same control table as C6, and not one gate computes it. Four switches are the opcode, a 74LS161 counts the six steps, and together they ADDRESS two EEPROMs whose contents ARE the control word. C6 needs new gates for every instruction and grows as instructions x states; this needs new bytes. That is why SAP-2, SAP-3 and every real CPU after them are microcoded. Note the counter: a one-hot ring says which state as a lit wire, and a ROM address wants a number.",
  "_category": "computer",
  "_difficulty": 5,
  "_stage": "C11"
